.TH MCXSTUDIO 1 "October 2026" "v2026 (3.0)" "MCX Studio man page"
.SH NAME
mcxstudio \- MCX Studio 2, a cross-platform GUI for MCX/MCX-CL/MMC simulations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mcxstudio
[\-\-run [\-\-quit]] [\-\-processor nvidia|amd|intel|cpu] [\-\-software]
[\-u/\-\-user] [input.json [result]]
.br
.B mcxstudio
\-\-snapshot out.png input.json [result]
.br
.B mcxstudio
\-p/\-\-project project.mcxp
.SH DESCRIPTION
MCX Studio is a graphical user interface (GUI) for MCX, MCX-CL and MMC
(https://mcx.space): set up a Monte Carlo photon transport simulation, run
it, and view the results. Starting from MCX v2026, the `mcxstudio` binary is
built from MCX Studio 2 (the mcxstudio2/ folder of the MCX source tree), a
rewrite of the original MCX Studio.

In MCX Studio 2, a simulation is the same .json input file that mcx, mcxcl
and mmc read; there is no separate project format. The Command tab shows the
command line that will be run, and the JSON tab shows the file that will be
written. The 3-D view renders the domain, shapes, mmc meshes, sources,
detectors, fluence volumes and photon trajectories using OpenGL 3.3, and
falls back to a software renderer when OpenGL 3.3 is not available. It can
read the JNIfTI/BNII (.jnii/.bnii), JData (.jdt/.jdb, legacy .jdat) and
mc2/NIfTI outputs written by the simulators.

The GUI itself does not contain any Monte Carlo modeling capability. It runs
externally compiled mcx (NVIDIA GPUs), mcx built with ROCm/HIP (AMD GPUs),
mcxcl (OpenCL CPUs/GPUs) or mmc (mesh-based, CPU/GPU) binaries; the choice
depends on the selected processor and the domain (voxel grid or mesh).

MCX Studio 2 is written in Object Pascal using Lazarus (2.2 or newer) and
Free Pascal (3.2), and runs on Windows, Linux and macOS.

The MCX/MCX-CL/MMC simulators are developed by Dr. Qianqian Fang at the
Computational Optics and Translational Imaging (COTI) Lab, at Northeastern
University, Boston, USA. More details can be found at https://mcx.space

.SH HOW TO USE
Start MCX Studio by typing "mcxstudio" in a terminal, or click on the
"MCX Studio" icon in the system menu. Open a .json input file, or start from
one of mcx's built-in benchmarks. The sections on the left follow the order
in which a simulation is described; pressing F1 over any setting shows what
it does and which mcx option it corresponds to. Click "Run" to start the
simulation; the output is written beside the input file.

An input that has no writable folder of its own, such as a new document or an
example installed under the program folder, is saved, together with the
volume or mesh files it refers to, to
MCXOutput/<simulator>sessions/<session>/ beside the program, or under the
home folder with \-\-user.

A simulator is searched in MCXSuite/<simulator>/bin beside the program,
beside the program, ~/MCXStudio, <simulator>/bin beside the program, the
source trees of the simulators and then the PATH.

Projects (.mcxp) created by the original MCX Studio can be opened with \-p,
or converted to .json files using utils/mcxp2json.py.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fBinput.json [result]\fR
Open a simulation input file; an optional second file (such as a .jnii,
\&.bnii or _traj.jdt output) is opened as the result beside the input.

.TP
\fB\-\-run\fR
Run the simulation once the window is up, as the Run button does.

.TP
\fB\-\-quit\fR
Used with \-\-run: close the window when the run ends; the simulator's output
is printed to stdout and its exit code becomes the program's exit code, so
that scripts and CI jobs can use the GUI's own run path.

.TP
\fB\-\-processor\fR \fInvidia|amd|intel|cpu\fR
What to run the simulation on; together with the domain, this selects mcx,
mcx on ROCm, mcxcl or mmc.

.TP
\fB\-\-snapshot\fR \fIout.png\fR
Write the 3-D view of the input (and of the result, if given) to a PNG file
and exit.

.TP
\fB\-\-software\fR
Draw the 3-D view on the processor instead of using OpenGL; the same can be
selected by setting MCXSTUDIO_SOFTWARE=1. The software renderer is used
anyway when OpenGL 3.3 is not available.

.TP
\fB\-p/\-\-project\fR \fIproject.mcxp\fR
Open a session of a project created by the original MCX Studio; the project
file can also be given directly as the input.

.TP
\fB\-u/\-\-user\fR
Keep the sessions that have no writable folder of their own under
~/MCXOutput rather than beside the program. This should be used when
mcxstudio is installed in a system folder; the Windows installer's shortcuts
start it this way.

.TP
\fB\-l/\-\-lang, \-r/\-\-registry\fR
Accepted for compatibility with the original MCX Studio and ignored.

.TP
\fB\-h/\-\-help\fR
Print the help information.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
    mcxstudio benchmark1.json benchmark1.jnii
    mcxstudio \-\-run \-\-processor intel benchmark1.json
    xvfb-run \-a mcxstudio \-\-run \-\-quit \-\-processor cpu onecube.json
    xvfb-run \-a mcxstudio \-\-snapshot qtest.png qtest.json qtest.jnii
.fi

